          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "Discharge Summary",
              "title": "ICU Discharge Summary",
              "author": "critical care",
              "date": "2026-03-09T13:20",
              "body": "BRIEF DISCHARGE SUMMARY (ICU / MEDICINE)\nAdmitted 01/11/2026 via the ED, discharged 01/17/2026.\n\nDiagnosis: delirium tremens.\nBackground: severe alcohol use disorder; chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency; alcoholic cerebellar degeneration; alcoholic liver disease. ED visit for withdrawal with confusion about two months earlier.\n\nPresentation: about 12 hours of mounting anxiety, hand tremor and heavy sweating after stopping alcohol abruptly; last drink roughly 36 hours before arrival. He had been drinking about a pint of vodka a day in the preceding months.\nBP 160/100, HR 120, RR 22, T 37.8 C. Agitated, diaphoretic, tremulous, disoriented to time and place. No focal neurologic deficit.\n\nLabs on arrival: Na 140, K 4.0, Cl 100, BUN 18, Cr 1.1, glucose 90, Mg 1.8, Ca 9.2, AST 72, ALT 50, alkaline phosphatase 90. CBC including platelets within normal limits.\n\nCourse: admitted to the ICU. Escalating benzodiazepines with adjunctive phenobarbital, high-dose IV thiamine, electrolyte repletion. Did not require intubation. Delirium cleared by day 4; transferred to the floor and discharged home on day 7.\n\nDischarge medications: home medications unchanged. Follow-up with primary care."
            }
          ]
