          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "Discharge Summary",
              "title": "Hospital Discharge Summary",
              "author": "hospital medicine",
              "date": "2026-07-23T09:45",
              "body": "DISCHARGE SUMMARY\nAdmitted 07/14/2026, discharged 07/20/2026.\n\nDiagnoses: community-acquired pneumonia; alcohol withdrawal seizures.\nBackground: severe alcohol use disorder with prior withdrawal including delirium tremens (01/2026); chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency; alcoholic cerebellar degeneration; alcoholic liver disease. NKDA.\n\nCourse: admitted with pneumonia on chest x-ray and started on IV ceftriaxone and oral azithromycin. Last alcohol about 24 hours before admission. On hospital day 2 (7/15) he grew agitated and tremulous and said he felt jittery. He denied headache, chest pain or breathlessness. T 37.8 C, BP 160/100, HR 110, RR 18. Diaphoretic, coarse tremor of the outstretched hands, anxious. Electrolytes, renal function and liver enzymes normal; blood alcohol undetectable. Later that day he had two brief generalized seizures, treated with IV lorazepam; symptom-triggered benzodiazepines thereafter. CT head showed no acute abnormality. No further seizures. Antiseizure medication not indicated for withdrawal seizures. Antibiotics completed (5 days) before discharge; afebrile, eating, ambulating at baseline.\n\nDischarge medications: home medications unchanged (pancrelipase, thiamine, folic acid, multivitamin).\n\nFollow-up: primary care in 1 to 2 weeks. Repeat chest x-ray in 6 to 8 weeks to document resolution. Strongly advised abstinence through a supervised program."
            }
